      "source": "本机 Codex ~/.codex/skills/verify-framework",
      "content": "---\nname: verify-framework\ndescription: Guide for working with the Verify framework, card heartbeat usage, return-data channels, local runtime protection, and Verify App local APK patching. Use when Codex needs to show developers how to call Heartbeat Runtime APIs, handle heartbeat callbacks and states, read heartbeat data, configure return channels, design layered local protection, compile or publish Verify cloud code, update resource zips, build or troubleshoot the NPatch-based local Patch runtime, query runtime crashes, inspect Dex cache isolation, install Persistent WiFi ADB Root, configure Dex entry points, operate a host app, inspect Verify logs, or diagnose class-loader, compile, and runtime failures.\n---\n\n# Verify Framework\n\n## Overview\n\nUse this skill for Verify cloud-code, runtime, device, MCP, and local Patch work. Load only the reference matching the current operation; detailed subjects are intentionally separated to reduce context use.\n\n## Reference Routing\n\n- Project identity, credentials, and token scope: `references/core-and-tokens.md`\n- Cloud-code compile and publish flow: `references/cloud-code-publish.md`\n- R8, Black, and compilation failures: `references/compile-troubleshooting.md`\n- `useLocalCache`, Dex lifecycle, and cache behavior: `references/dex-cache-runtime.md`\n- Reusing one Dex across multiple configs: `references/multi-config-publishing.md`\n- Resource zip, native files, and ITHook module download: `references/resources-and-modules.md`\n- MenuPro cloud-code integration: `references/menupro-integration.md`\n- Persistent WiFi ADB Root installation: `references/wifi-adb-root.md`\n- Verify MCP tool selection: `references/mcp-tools.md`\n- Runtime crash query, event analysis, and report download: `references/runtime-crash-analysis.md`\n- Device launch, logs, traces, crashes, and dumps: `references/device-debugging.md`\n- Verify App local Patch, embedded Runtime, and Dex health isolation: `references/local-patch-runtime.md`\n- Card heartbeat developer usage, callbacks, states, and return channels: `references/heartbeat-verification.md`\n- Layered local protection, leases, Java/JavaScript handshakes, and low-noise integrity checks: `references/local-protection.md`\n- Workspace-specific examples: `references/project-examples.md`\n- ShadowHook integration: use the separate `verify-shadowhook` skill.\n\n`references/verify-framework-guide.md` is a compact index for compatibility with older prompts that cite the original guide.\n\n## Operating Rules\n\n- Confirm `configId`, package name, workspace, entry class, entry method, and runtime before publishing.\n- Preserve unrelated dirty-worktree changes.\n- Push changed resource zips before the Dex that consumes them.\n- Push Dex after Java cloud-code changes and verify the returned Hash.\n- Treat `useLocalCache` as host-runtime Dex persistence, not compiler caching or deterministic output.\n- Keep `Template/verfiy_hook_embedded`, `Template/verfiy_hook`, and `Template/verfiy_inject` as separate products.\n- Treat heartbeat as a developer-initiated, time-card-only Runtime API. Ordinary verification and heartbeat remain independent developer choices.\n- For local Patch, rebuild and synchronize `loader.bin`; a source-only edit does not update Verify App assets.\n- Prefer project cloud-code/resource changes over framework APK changes unless the framework itself is the requested target.\n\n## Token Handling\n\nVerify uses two credential scopes:\n\n- `VERIFY_API_TOKEN`: apps, configs, source, cloud-code, resources, cards, and releases.\n- `VERIFY_PROTECT_TOKEN`: VMP, obfuscation, Dex2C, SO protection, and protect tasks.\n\nUse `mcp__verify.server_info` to inspect runtime token sources. Set a supplied token with `mcp__verify.set_api_token` or `mcp__verify.set_protect_token`, then retry the original operation. Read `references/core-and-tokens.md` for exact handling.\n\n## Cloud Workflow\n\n1. Inspect config and source assumptions.\n2. Edit cloud-code and resource inputs.\n3. Validate entry class/method and runtime API usage.\n4. Publish changed resource zip first.\n5. Compile and apply cloud-code Dex.\n6. Restart or launch the host.\n7. Verify process state, build label, Hash, runtime logs, and generated files.\n\nRead the cloud, resource, and device references for concrete payloads and commands.\n\n## Local Patch Workflow\n\n1. Edit embedded Runtime only under `Template/verfiy_hook_embedded`.\n2. Run focused Runtime tests.\n3. Run `verfiy_app/npatch-engine/sync-runtime.sh`.\n4. Verify source and bundled `loader.bin` hashes match.\n5. Build Verify App or patch a fixture APK, then inspect host Runtime logs.\n\nThe cached Dex health guard uses a 10-second window and a same-Hash two-strike threshold. Read `references/local-patch-runtime.md` before changing marker, restart, retry, or quarantine behavior.\n\n## Verification Baseline\n\nAfter any publish, build, install, or patch operation, verify the relevant subset:\n\n- expected artifact exists and has a recorded SHA-256\n- requested ABIs are packaged\n- patched or host process remains alive\n- logs show the new build label and Dex Hash\n- resource/native paths resolve to the current version\n- Java/native crash evidence and app-private reports agree\n- output APK parses and its signature verifies\n\nWhen Verify MCP tools are absent from the active session, provide the exact intended payload and continue with local source/build/device checks that are available.\n",
      "enabled": true,
      "built_in": true,
